ample": {"task": "The graph shows Maxwell–Boltzmann curves at two temperatures. In required practical 3, a cross viewed through sodium thiosulfate and acid disappears faster when hotter. Explain why, in terms of collisions.", "steps": [{"do": "At higher temperature the particles have more energy. The Maxwell–Boltzmann peak moves right and lower, and the area under the curve stays the same.", "why": "Describes the distribution change. The same area means the same number of particles."}, {"do": "The activation energy is unchanged, so a much larger fraction of particles has energy ≥ Ea.", "why": "This is the main cause. Ea is fixed for the reaction."}, {"do": "So a greater proportion of collisions are successful.", "why": "Links particle energy to collisions that actually react."}, {"do": "Particles also collide slightly more often, so there are more successful collisions per second and the rate increases.", "why": "Rate is successful collisions per unit time. This earns the link to rate."}, {"do": "Sulfur forms faster, so the cross disappears sooner and the time is shorter.", "why": "Ties the theory back to the measurement in the practical."}], "answer": "Higher temperature moves the peak right and lower with the same area. A much larger fraction of particles has energy ≥ Ea, so more collisions succeed per second. The rate rises, sulfur forms faster and the cross disappears sooner.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 8], "y": [0, 0.6], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 0.1, "xlabel": "Energy", "ylabel": "Number of molecules", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.05, 0.048361], [0.1, 0.093551], [0.15, 0.135726], [0.2, 0.175035], [0.25, 0.21162], [0.3, 0.245619], [0.35, 0.277161], [0.4, 0.306371], [0.45, 0.333368], [0.5, 0.358266], [0.55, 0.381172], [0.6, 0.402192], [0.65, 0.421424], [0.7, 0.438962], [0.75, 0.454898], [0.8, 0.469317], [0.85, 0.482302], [0.9, 0.49393], [0.95, 0.504278], [1.0, 0.513417], [1.05, 0.521415], [1.1, 0.528336], [1.15, 0.534243], [1.2, 0.539195], [1.25, 0.54324