{"s": "further", "t": "fp-matrices", "kind": "method", "why": "Matrix questions appear on Papers 1 and 2, and determinants, inverses and eigenvectors are multi-step methods where each correct step earns marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Determinant and inverse", "b": "For $\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$, the **determinant** is $ad-bc$. The **inverse** is $\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{pmatrix}$. If $\\det=0$ the matrix is **singular**: it has no inverse."}, {"h": "What the determinant tells you", "b": "The determinant is the **area scale factor** of the transformation (volume for a 3×3 matrix). A negative value means **orientation** is reversed. Drag the slider on the graph: at det = 0 the image is flat. If $\\det\\neq0$, $\\mathbf{Ax}=\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$. Also $(\\mathbf{AB})^{-1}=\\mathbf{B}^{-1}\\mathbf{A}^{-1}$.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [-2, 5], "xstep": 1, "y": [-1, 3], "ystep": 1, "equal": true, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"dash": true, "label": "Unit square", "c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0], [1, 1], [0, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 1], "label": "j′"}], "frames": [{"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [-1.0, 1], [0.0, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [-1.0, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = −1", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = −2", "Area of image = |det A| = 2"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [-0.5, 1], [0.5, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [-0.5, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = −0.5", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = −1.5", "Area of image = |det A| = 1.5"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [0.0, 1], [1.0, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.0, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 0", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = −1", "Area of image = |det A| = 1"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [0.5, 1], [1.5, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [0.5, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 0.5", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = −0.5", "Area of image = |det A| = 0.5"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [1.0, 1], [2.0, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1.0, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 1", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = 0", "Area of image = |det A| = 0"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [1.5, 1], [2.5, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1.5, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 1.5", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = 0.5", "Area of image = |det A| = 0.5"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [2.0, 1], [3.0, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [2.0, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 2", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = 1", "Area of image = |det A| = 1"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [2.5, 1], [3.5, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [2.5, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 2.5", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = 1.5", "Area of image = |det A| = 1.5"]}, {"lines": [{"label": "Image", "c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [3.0, 1], [4.0, 2], [1, 1], [0, 0]]}], "points": [{"at": [3.0, 1], "label": "i′"}], "read": ["A = [k 1; 1 1], k = 3", "det A = k×1 − 1×1 = 2", "Area of image = |det A| = 2"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "k", "vals": ["−1", "−0.5", "0", "0.5", "1", "1.5", "2", "2.5", "3"], "start": 8}}}, {"h": "Finding eigenvalues", "b": "An **eigenvector** $\\mathbf{v}$ keeps its direction: $\\mathbf{Av}=\\lambda\\mathbf{v}$, where $\\lambda$ is the **eigenvalue**. Rearranging gives $(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$. Non-zero solutions need $\\det(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})=0$, the **characteristic equation**."}, {"h": "Finding eigenvectors", "b": "Substitute each **eigenvalue** $\\lambda$ into $(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$. For a 2×2 matrix the two equations are multiples of each other, so use one. Any **non-zero multiple** of your vector is also correct."}], "example": {"task": "Find the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\\\2&2\\end{pmatrix}$ and a corresponding eigenvector for each. Drag the slider on the graph to move P = (1, m); P is an eigenvector when AP lies along OP.", "steps": [{"do": "Form $\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I}=\\begin{pmatrix}3-\\lambda&1\\\\2&2-\\lambda\\end{pmatrix}$ and set its determinant to zero.", "why": "Non-zero eigenvectors exist only when $\\det(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})=0$."}, {"do": "$(3-\\lambda)(2-\\lambda)-2=0$, so $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0$.", "why": "Expand carefully: $6-5\\lambda+\\lambda^2-2$."}, {"do": "$(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda-4)=0$, so $\\lambda=1$ or $\\lambda=4$.", "why": "Factorise the quadratic to get both eigenvalues."}, {"do": "For $\\lambda=1$: $\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives $2x+y=0$. Take $x=1$, $y=-2$.", "why": "Both rows give the same equation. One equation fixes the direction."}, {"do": "For $\\lambda=4$: $\\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\\\2&-2\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives $-x+y=0$. Take $x=1$, $y=1$.", "why": "Again the rows are multiples, so $y=x$ is enough."}, {"do": "Check: $\\mathbf{A}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}=4\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$.", "why": "Confirms $\\mathbf{Av}=\\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ and catches arithmetic slips."}], "answer": "$\\lambda=1$ with $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-2\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\lambda=4$ with $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$ (or any non-zero multiples).", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [-2, 7], "xstep": 1, "y": [-8, 10], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 2, "pts": [[1, -4], [1, 3]]}], "points": [], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -4.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [-1.0, -6.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -4.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [-1.0, -6.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −4), AP = (−1, −6)", "gradient of OP = −4", "gradient of O to AP = 6"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -3.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [-0.5, -5.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -3.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [-0.5, -5.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −3.5), AP = (−0.5, −5)", "gradient of OP = −3.5", "gradient of O to AP = 10"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -2.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [0.5, -3.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -2.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [0.5, -3.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −2.5), AP = (0.5, −3)", "gradient of OP = −2.5", "gradient of O to AP = −6"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -2.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [1.0, -2.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -2.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [1.0, -2.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −2), AP = (1, −2)", "gradient of OP = −2", "gradient of O to AP = −2"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -1.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [1.5, -1.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -1.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [1.5, -1.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −1.5), AP = (1.5, −1)", "gradient of OP = −1.5", "gradient of O to AP = −0.67"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -1.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [2.0, 0.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -1.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [2.0, 0.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −1), AP = (2, 0)", "gradient of OP = −1", "gradient of O to AP = 0"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, -0.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [2.5, 1.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -0.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [2.5, 1.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, −0.5), AP = (2.5, 1)", "gradient of OP = −0.5", "gradient of O to AP = 0.4"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [3.0, 2.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 0.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [3.0, 2.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 0), AP = (3, 2)", "gradient of OP = 0", "gradient of O to AP = 0.67"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [3.5, 3.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 0.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [3.5, 3.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 0.5), AP = (3.5, 3)", "gradient of OP = 0.5", "gradient of O to AP = 0.86"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 1.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [4.0, 4.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 1.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [4.0, 4.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 1), AP = (4, 4)", "gradient of OP = 1", "gradient of O to AP = 1"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 1.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [4.5, 5.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 1.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [4.5, 5.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 1.5), AP = (4.5, 5)", "gradient of OP = 1.5", "gradient of O to AP = 1.11"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 2.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [5.0, 6.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 2.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [5.0, 6.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 2), AP = (5, 6)", "gradient of OP = 2", "gradient of O to AP = 1.2"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 2.5]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [5.5, 7.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 2.5], "label": "P"}, {"at": [5.5, 7.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 2.5), AP = (5.5, 7)", "gradient of OP = 2.5", "gradient of O to AP = 1.27"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0, 0], [1, 3.0]]}, {"c": 1, "pts": [[0, 0], [6.0, 8.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, 3.0], "label": "P"}, {"at": [6.0, 8.0], "label": "AP"}], "read": ["P = (1, 3), AP = (6, 8)", "gradient of OP = 3", "gradient of O to AP = 1.33"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "m", "vals": ["−4", "−3.5", "−2.5", "−2", "−1.5", "−1", "−0.5", "0", "0.5", "1", "1.5", "2", "2.5", "3"], "start": 9}}}, "selfx": {"q": "In the λ = 1 step, why is it enough to use only the equation 2x + y = 0?", "o": ["The second row is only needed when the eigenvalue is negative", "Because det(A − λI) = 0, the second row gives an equivalent equation", "Eigenvectors depend only on the first row of the matrix", "The second row gives x = 0, which is not allowed for an eigenvector"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong: the sign of the eigenvalue makes no difference. Both rows are always used in forming the equations.", "Right: the determinant being zero means the rows of A − λI are multiples, so one equation carries all the information.", "Wrong: both rows of A − λI matter. Here they happen to give the same equation, because the determinant is zero.", "Wrong: the second row is $2x+y=0$ too, not $x=0$. A zero component would not be forbidden anyway; only the zero vector is."]}, "faded": {"task": "Find an eigenvector of $\\mathbf{B}=\\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\\\3&2\\end{pmatrix}$ for the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$.", "given": ["$\\det(\\mathbf{B}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})=(4-\\lambda)(2-\\lambda)-3=\\lambda^2-6\\lambda+5$", "$(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda-5)=0$, so $\\lambda=1$ or $\\lambda=5$"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$9x+y=0$, so $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-9\\end{pmatrix}$", "$-x+y=0$, so $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$", "$-x+y=0$, so $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$", "$3x+y=0$, so $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}$"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong: this uses $\\mathbf{B}+5\\mathbf{I}$. You must subtract $\\lambda$ from the leading diagonal, giving $4-5=-1$.", "Wrong: $-x+y=0$ gives $y=x$, not $y=-x$. Rearrange the sign carefully.", "Right: $\\mathbf{B}-5\\mathbf{I}=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\\\3&-3\\end{pmatrix}$, so $-x+y=0$ and $y=x$. Then $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$.", "Wrong: this is the eigenvector for $\\lambda=1$, from $\\mathbf{B}-\\mathbf{I}$. The question asks about $\\lambda=5$."]}, "trap": "Writing $(\\mathbf{AB})^{-1}=\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\mathbf{B}^{-1}$ instead of $\\mathbf{B}^{-1}\\mathbf{A}^{-1}$, or swapping the wrong entries: swap $a$ and $d$, negate $b$ and $c$. For eigenvalues, subtract $\\lambda$ only from the leading diagonal.", "recap": "Inverse: swap $a$ and $d$, negate $b$ and $c$, divide by $\\det$; eigenvectors: solve $\\det(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})=0$, then $(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$."}
{"s": "further", "t": "fp-num", "kind": "method", "why": "Exam questions ask you to apply these rules accurately, and method marks come from writing the formula with correct values substituted before rounding.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Mid-ordinate rule", "b": "Split $[a,b]$ into $n$ strips of width $h=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}$. Read $y$ at the **mid-ordinate** (the middle) of each strip. Each strip becomes a rectangle, so $\\int_a^b y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\approx h\\,(y_{1/2}+y_{3/2}+\\dots+y_{n-1/2})$. This is the **mid-ordinate rule**: add the midpoint heights, then multiply by $h$."}, {"h": "Simpson's rule", "b": "Use an **even** number of strips $n$, which gives $n+1$ ordinates $y_0,\\dots,y_n$. Then $\\int_a^b y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\approx\\dfrac{h}{3}\\left[y_0+y_n+4(\\text{odd})+2(\\text{even})\\right]$. The **odd** ordinates are $y_1,y_3,\\dots$ and the even interior ones are $y_2,y_4,\\dots$. The **weights** run $1,4,2,4,\\dots,4,1$."}, {"h": "Euler's method", "b": "For $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=f(x,y)$ with a known start $(x_0,y_0)$, move in steps of **step length** $h$. Use $x_{r+1}=x_r+h$ and $y_{r+1}=y_r+h\\,f(x_r,y_r)$. Each step follows the **gradient** at its start point along a straight line. Press Next step to build the graph for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=y$, $y=1$ at $x=0$, $h=0.5$. The last step shows $h=0.25$: a smaller $h$ usually gives a better estimate.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "steps", "x": [0, 1.5], "y": [0, 4.5], "xstep": 0.25, "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 1.0], [0.0094, 1.0094], [0.0187, 1.0189], [0.0281, 1.0285], [0.0375, 1.0382], [0.0469, 1.048], [0.0563, 1.0579], [0.0656, 1.0678], [0.075, 1.0779], [0.0844, 1.088], [0.0938, 1.0983], [0.1031, 1.1086], [0.1125, 1.1191], [0.1219, 1.1296], [0.1313, 1.1403], [0.1406, 1.151], [0.15, 1.1618], [0.1594, 1.1728], [0.1688, 1.1838], [0.1781, 1.195], [0.1875, 1.2062], [0.1969, 1.2176], [0.20