arate factor and does not cancel it."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows the spiral r=θ for 0≤θ≤π. Find the area enclosed by the spiral and the initial line.", "given": ["Use $A=\\frac12\\int_0^{\\pi} r^2\\,d\\theta$.", "Substitute $r=\\theta$: $A=\\frac12\\int_0^{\\pi}\\theta^2\\,d\\theta$."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$A=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{\\theta^2}{2}\\right]_0^{\\pi}=\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}$", "$A=\\left[\\frac{\\theta^3}{3}\\right]_0^{\\pi}=\\frac{\\pi^3}{3}$", "$A=\\frac12\\left[2\\theta\\right]_0^{\\pi}=\\pi$", "$A=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{\\theta^3}{3}\\right]_0^{\\pi}=\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}$"], "a": 3, "f": ["This integrates $\\theta$, not $\\theta^2$. The integrand is already $r^2=\\theta^2$, so the antiderivative is $\\frac{\\theta^3}{3}$.", "This drops the factor $\\frac12$ from the area formula, so the answer is twice too big.", "This differentiates $\\theta^2$ instead of integrating it. The area needs the antiderivative $\\frac{\\theta^3}{3}$.", "Correct. $\\int\\theta^2\\,d\\theta=\\frac{\\theta^3}{3}$. Keep the $\\frac12$, then substitute $\\pi$ and $0$ to get $\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}$."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-4, 2], "y": [-1, 3], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "equal": true, "lines": [{"pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.017451, 0.000305], [0.034885, 0.001218], [0.052288, 0.00274], [0.069643, 0.00487], [0.086934, 0.007606], [0.104146, 0.010946], [0.121262, 0.014889], [0.138268, 0.019432], [0.155146, 0.024573], [0.171881, 0.030307], [0.188459, 0.036633], [0.204863, 0.043545], [0.221078, 0.05104], [0.237088, 0.059113], [0.252879, 0.067759], [0.268435, 0.076972], [0.283741, 0.086748], [0.298783, 0.097081], [0.313546, 0.107962], [0.328015, 0.119388], [0.342175, 0.131349], [0.356013, 0.143839], [0.369514, 0.15685], [0.382665, 0.170373], [0.395451, 0.184402], [0.40786, 0.198927], [0.419877, 0.213938], [0.43149, 0.229427], [0.442685, 0.245384], [0.45345, 0.261799], [0.463772, 0.278662], [0.473639, 0.295963], [0.48304, 0.31369], [0.49196