 [12.64, 4.744], [12.831, 4.471], [13.023, 4.189], [13.214, 3.896], [13.406, 3.594], [13.597, 3.28], [13.789, 2.957], [13.98, 2.623], [14.172, 2.279], [14.363, 1.925], [14.555, 1.561], [14.746, 1.186], [14.938, 0.801], [15.129, 0.406], [15.321, 0.0]], "label": "path"}], "points": [{"at": [15.3209, 0], "label": "Lands"}], "read": ["u cos α = 5.92, u sin α = 12.69", "Time of flight = 2.59 s", "Range = 20 sin 2α = 15.32 m"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.161, 0.436], [0.321, 0.861], [0.482, 1.275], [0.643, 1.678], [0.803, 2.07], [0.964, 2.45], [1.125, 2.82], [1.286, 3.179], [1.446, 3.527], [1.607, 3.863], [1.768, 4.189], [1.928, 4.503], [2.089, 4.807], [2.25, 5.099], [2.41, 5.381], [2.571, 5.651], [2.732, 5.911], [2.893, 6.159], [3.053, 6.396], [3.214, 6.623], [3.375, 6.838], [3.535, 7.042], [3.696, 7.235], [3.857, 7.417], [4.017, 7.588], [4.178, 7.749], [4.339, 7.898], [4.5, 8.036], [4.66, 8.162], [4.821, 8.278], [4.982, 8.383], [5.142, 8.477], [5.303, 8.56], [5.464, 8.632], [5.624, 8.692], [5.785, 8.742], [5.946, 8.781], [6.106, 8.808], [6.267, 8.825], [6.428, 8.83], [6.589, 8.825], [6.749, 8.808], [6.91, 8.781], [7.071, 8.742], [7.231, 8.692], [7.392, 8.632], [7.553, 8.56], [7.713, 8.477], [7.874, 8.383], [8.035, 8.278], [8.196, 8.162], [8.356, 8.036], [8.517, 7.898], [8.678, 7.749], [8.838, 7.588], [8.999, 7.417], [9.16, 7.235], [9.32, 7.042], [9.481, 6.838], [9.642, 6.623], [9.803, 6.396], [9.963, 6.159], [10.124, 5.911], [10.285, 5.651], [10.445, 5.381], [10.606, 5.099], [10.767, 4.807], [10.927, 4.503], [11.088, 4.189], [11.249, 3.863], [11.409, 3.527], [11.57, 3.179], [11.731, 2.82], [11.892, 2.45], [12.052, 2.07], [12.213, 1.678], [12.374, 1.275], [12.534, 0.861], [12.695, 0.436], [12.856, 0.0]], "label": "path"}], "points": [{"at": [12.8558, 0], "label": "Lands"}], "read": ["u cos α = 4.79, u sin α = 13.16", "Time of flight = 2.68 s", "Range = 20 sin 2α = 12.86 m"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.125, 0.461], [0.25, 0.91], [0.375, 1.347], [0.5, 1.773], [0.625, 2.187], [0.75, 2.589], [0.875, 2.98], [1.0, 3.359], [1.125, 3.726], [1.25, 4.082], [1.375, 4.426], [1.5, 4.758], [1.625, 5.079], [1.75, 5.388], [1.875, 5.686], [2.0, 5.971], [2.125, 6.245], [2.25, 6.508], [2.375, 6.759], [2.5, 6.998], [2.625, 7.225], [2.75, 7.441], [2.875, 7.645], [3.0, 7.837], [3.125, 8.018], [3.25, 8.187], [3.375, 8.345], [3.5, 8.49], [3.625, 8.625], [3.75, 8.747], [3.875, 8.858], [4.0, 8.957], [4.125, 9.044], [4.25, 9.12], [4.375, 9.184], [4.5, 9.237], [4.625, 9.278], [4.75, 9.307], [4.875, 9.324], [5.0, 9.33], [5.125, 9.324], [5.25, 9.307], [5.375, 9.278], [5.5, 9.237], [5.625, 9.184], [5.75, 9.12], [5.875, 9.044], [6.0, 8.957], [6.125, 8.858], [6.25, 8.747], [6.375, 8.625], [6.5, 8.49], [6.625, 8.345], [6.75, 8.187], [6.875, 8.018], [7.0, 7.837], [7.125, 7.645], [7.25, 7.441], [7.375, 7.225], [7.5, 6.998], [7.625, 6.759], [7.75, 6.508], [7.875, 6.245], [8.0, 5.971], [8.125, 5.686], [8.25, 5.388], [8.375, 5.079], [8.5, 4.758], [8.625, 4.426], [8.75, 4.082], [8.875, 3.726], [9.0, 3.359], [9.125, 2.98], [9.25, 2.589], [9.375, 2.187], [9.5, 1.773], [9.625, 1.347], [9.75, 0.91], [9.875, 0.461], [10.0, 0.0]], "label": "path"}], "points": [{"at": [10.0, 0], "label": "Lands"}], "read": ["u cos α = 3.62, u sin α = 13.52", "Time of flight = 2.76 s", "Range = 20 sin 2α = 10 m"]}], "ctl": {"type": "slider", "label": "a", "vals": ["15", "20", "25", "30", "35", "40", "45", "50", "55", "60", "65", "70", "75"], "start": 3}}}, {"h": "Velocity and path", "b": "At time $t$, find $v_x$ and $v_y$. The **speed** is $\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}$ and the direction comes from $\\tan\\theta=\\frac{|v_y|}{v_x}$. To get the **path**, eliminate $t$: $y=x\\tan\\alpha-\\dfrac{gx^2}{2u^2\\cos^2\\alpha}$, a downward parabola."}], "example": {"task": "A ball is thrown from the top of a tower 24.5 m above level ground at 24.5 m s⁻¹ at angle α above the horizontal, where $\\sin\\alpha=\\frac45$. Find the horizontal distance to where it lands. Take $g=9.8$.", "steps": [{"do": "Since $\\sin\\alpha=\\frac45$, $\\cos\\alpha=\\frac35$. Horizontal component: $24.5\\times\\frac35=14.7$. Vertical component: $24.5\\times\\frac45=19.6$.", "why": "Resolving first gives the two independent starting velocities."}, {"do": "Vertical, up positive: $u=19.6$, $a=-9.8$, $s=-24.5$, $t$ unknown.", "why": "The ground is 24.5 m below the start, so the displacement is negative."}, {"do": "Use $s=ut+\\frac12at^2$: $-24.5=19.6t-4.9t^2$. Rearrange to $4.9t^2-19.6t-24.5=0$, so $t^2-4t-5=0$.", "why": "This is a quadratic in $t$; dividing by 4.9 makes it easy to solve."}, {"do": "Factorise: $(t-5)(t+1)=0$. So $t=5$ s, rejecting $t=-1$.", "why": "Time of flight cannot be negative."}, {"do": "Horizontal: $x=14.7\\times5=73.5$ m.", "why": "Horizontal acceleration is zero (no air resistance), so velocity is constant and the same $t$ applies."}], "answer": "The ball lands 73.5 m horizontally from the tower, after a flight time of 5 s.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 85.75], "y": [0, 49], "xstep": 12.25, "ystep": 12.25, "xlabel": "horizontal distance x / m", "ylabel": "height y / m", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 24.5], [10, 35.57], [20, 42.1], [29.4, 44.1], [40, 41.55], [50, 34.48], [60, 22.87], [70, 6.72], [73.5, 0]], "label": "path", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [0, 24.5], "label": "Start"}, {"at": [73.5, 0], "label": "Lands"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is $s=-24.5$ used in the vertical equation?", "o": ["The ball is slowing down as it moves upwards, so $s$ must be negative", "Gravity acts downwards, so every quantity in the equation is negative", "The ball lands with a downward speed of 24.5 m s⁻¹", "With up as positive, the ball finishes 24.5 m below where it started"], "a": 3, "f": ["Slowing down affects the sign of acceleration, not displacement. Displacement is final position minus start position.", "Only $a$ is negative here. $u=19.6$ is upwards and stays positive. Each quantity takes its sign from the chosen direction.", "24.5 is the launch speed and the tower height. Neither is the landing speed. Displacement relates to position.", "Correct: displacement is final minus start. The ground is 24.5 m below the launch point and up is positive, so $s=-24.5$."]}, "faded": {"task": "A stone is thrown horizontally at 8 m s⁻¹ from a cliff top 44.1 m above the sea. Find the horizontal distance travelled before it hits the sea. Take $g=9.8$.", "given": ["Horizontal: $v_x=8$, constant.", "Vertical (up positive): $u=0$, $a=-9.8$, $s=-44.1$."], "q": "What is the correct next step?", "o": ["$-44.1=8t-4.9t^2$, using the horizontal speed as $u$ in the vertical equation", "$-44.1=0-4.9t^2$, so $t^2=9$ and $t=3$ s", "$-44.1=-9.8t^2$, so $t=\\sqrt{4.5}\\approx2.12$ s", "$-44.1=0-4.9t$, so $t=9$ s"], "a": 1, "f": ["The 8 m s⁻¹ is horizontal. The initial vertical velocity is 0, so it cannot appear in the vertical equation.", "Correct: with $u=0$, $s=\\frac12at^2$ gives $-44.1=-4.9t^2$, so $t=3$. Then $x=8\\times3=24$ m.", "This drops the $\\frac12$ from $\\frac12at^2$. Half of 9.8 is 4.9, not 9.8.", "This drops the square on $t$. The term is $\\frac12at^2$, not $\\frac12at$."]}, "trap": "Using the full launch speed, or the wrong component, in a suvat equation, or giving $s$ the wrong sign. Resolve first, state \"up positive\", and share only $t$ between the two directions.", "recap": "Resolve the velocity, find the time from the vertical motion with careful signs, then use that time in the constant-velocity horizontal motion."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "m2-07", "kind": "method", "why": "Multi-mark mechanics questions in Paper 2 often combine slopes, friction, pulleys or equilibrium, and they all start from the same method: resolve, then apply Newton's second law.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Resolve in two directions", "b": "Draw every force on the particle. Choose two perpendicular directions, usually **along** and **perpendicular to** the surface. Resolve each force into them. Perpendicular to the surface there is no acceleration, so the forces balance. Along it, **resultant force $=ma$**."}, {"h": "Slopes and friction", "b": "On a slope at angle $\\theta$, weight has components $mg\\sin\\theta$ down the slope and $mg\\cos\\theta$ into it. So $R=mg\\cos\\theta$ when nothing else acts perpendicular. **Friction** opposes motion, and $F=\\mu R$ (**limiting friction**) when the particle is moving or about to move."}, {"h": "Connected particles", "b": "Write a separate equation of motion for each particle. A **light inextensible string** gives the same **tension** throughout and the same acceleration for both particles. A **smooth pulley** changes the string's direction but not its tension. Add the equations to eliminate $T$."}, {"h": "Equilibrium in two dimensions", "b": "If a particle is in **equilibrium**, the resultant force is zero. Resolve in two **perpendicular directions** and set each total to zero. This gives two equations, enough to find two unknown forces or an unknown angle."}], "example": {"task": "A 3 kg particle A lies on a rough horizontal table ($\\mu=0.4$). A light string over a smooth pulley at the table edge joins it to a 2 kg particle B hanging freely. Find $a$ and $T$. ($g=9.8$)", "steps": [{"do": "Resolve vertically for A: $R=3g=29.4$ N.", "why": "A has no vertical acceleration, so $R$ balances its weight."}, {"do": "Friction $F=\\mu R=0.4\\times29.4=11.76$ N, acting away from the pulley. B's weight is 19.6 N, which is greater, so the system moves.", "why": "Motion occurs, so friction is at its limiting value and opposes motion."}, {"do": "B, taking downwards as positive: $19.6-T=2a$.", "why": "Resultant force $=ma$ for B, with weight down and tension up."}, {"do": "A, taking towards the pulley as positive: $T-11.76=3a$.", "why": "Tension pulls A forwards and friction opposes. A has the same acceleration as B."}, {"do": "Add the equations: $19.6-11.76=5a$, so $a=7.84\\div5=1.568$.", "why": "$T$ cancels, leaving one equation in $a$."}, {"do": "Substitute into B's equation: $T=19.6-2(1.568)=16.464$.", "why": "With $a$ known, $T$ is the only unknown. A's equation gives $11.76+3(1.568)=16.464$ too, which checks it."}], "answer": "$a\\approx1.57$ m s⁻² and $T\\approx16.5$ N."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why can the same tension $T$ be used in the equations for A and for B?", "o": ["Both particles have weights that are nearly equal, so the forces match", "The string is light and the pulley is smooth, so tension is the same throughout", "Friction on A is limiting, so the tension must equal friction", "The tension always equals the weight of the hanging particle B"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong: the weights are not equal here (29.4 N against 19.6 N). The tension is the same because of the modelling assumptions, not the masses.", "Right: a light string and a smooth pulley mean the tension is the same on both sides of the pulley, so one symbol $T$ serves both equations.", "Wrong: if $T$ equalled friction, A would not accelerate. Friction is only one of the forces in A's equation.", "Wrong: $T$ equals B's weight only if B is in equilibrium. Here B accelerates, so $T<19.6$ N."]}, "faded": {"task": "A 5 kg box on rough horizontal ground ($\\mu=0.5$) is pulled by a rope with tension 30 N at angle $\\alpha$ above the horizontal, $\\sin\\alpha=0.6$, $\\cos\\alpha=0.8$. Find its acceleration. ($g=9.8$)", "given": ["Resolve vertically: $R+30\\times0.6=5\\times9.8$, so $R=31$ N.", "The box moves, so $F=\\mu R=0.5\\times31$."], "q": "Which is the correct equation of motion horizontally?", "o": ["$30\\times0.6-0.5\\times31=5a$", "$30-0.5\\times31=5a$", "$30\\times0.8-0.5\\times31=5a$", "$30\\times0.8-0.5\\times49=5a$"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong: this uses $\\sin\\alpha$. The horizontal component of the tension is $30\\cos\\alpha$, because $\\alpha$ is measured from the horizontal.", "Wrong: this ignores the angle and uses the full 30 N horizontally. Only the component $30\\cos\\alpha=24$ N acts horizontally.", "Right: the horizontal component is $30\\times0.8=24$ N, and friction is $0.5\\times31=15.5$ N. So $8.5=5a$ and $a=1.7$ m s⁻².", "Wrong: this uses $R=mg=49$ N. The rope's upward component reduces $R$ to 31 N, so friction is smaller."]}, "trap": "Assuming $R=mg$ every time. On a slope or with an angled force, find $R$ by resolving perpendicular to the surface. Also remember friction acts against the motion.", "recap": "Resolve along and perpendicular to the surface, use $F=ma$ for each particle, and use $F=\\mu R$ with $R$ found by resolving."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "m2-08", "kind": "method", "why": "Mechanics questions on 2D motion link position, velocity and acceleration in several marks, and one missing constant of integration spoils every later answer.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Differentiate to go down", "b": "Velocity is the **rate of change** of position, and acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. So $\\mathbf{v}=\\dfrac{d\\mathbf{r}}{dt}$ and $\\mathbf{a}=\\dfrac{d\\mathbf{v}}{dt}$. Differentiate the i and j **components** separately. Substitute the value of $t$ only after differentiating."}, {"h": "Integrate to go up", "b": "To go from $\\mathbf{a}$ to $\\mathbf{v}$, or from $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{r}$, integrate each component. Each integration adds a **constant vector**, so each component gets its own constant. Find the constants from the **initial conditions**, such as the velocity or position at $t=0$."}, {"h": "Constant acceleration only", "b": "The vector forms $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{a}t$ and $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}_0+\\mathbf{u}t+\\tfrac12\\mathbf{a}t^2$ are valid only when $\\mathbf{a}$ is **constant**. If $\\mathbf{a}$ contains $t$, you must **integrate**. Check the acceleration before choosing a method."}, {"h": "Reading the conditions", "b": "**Speed** is the magnitude of velocity: $\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}$. \"Moving **parallel** to i\" means the j-component of velocity is zero. \"**At rest**\" means both components are zero. Turn each phrase into an equation for $t$."}], "example": {"task": "Acceleration (6t i − 4j) m s⁻²; at t = 0, v = (2i + 8j) m s⁻¹, r = (i − j) m. Find r when moving parallel to i. Press Play on the graph and watch the velocity arrow.", "steps": [{"do": "Integrate the acceleration: $\\mathbf{v}=(3t^2+c_1)\\mathbf{i}+(-4t+c_2)\\mathbf{j}$.", "why": "Acceleration depends on $t$, so integrate each component. Each component has its own constant."}, {"do": "Use $\\mathbf{v}=2\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}$ at $t=0$: $c_1=2$, $c_2=8$. So $\\mathbf{v}=(3t^2+2)\\mathbf{i}+(8-4t)\\mathbf{j}$.", "why": "At $t=0$ the $t$ terms vanish, so the constants equal the initial components."}, {"do": "Parallel to $\\mathbf{i}$ means $8-4t=0$, so $t=2$.", "why": "A vector parallel to $\\mathbf{i}$ has zero j-component. This gives the time."}, {"do": "Integrate the velocity: $\\mathbf{r}=(t^3+2t+d_1)\\mathbf{i}+(8t-2t^2+d_2)\\mathbf{j}$.", "why": "Position is the integral of velocity. Two new constants appear."}, {"do": "Use $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}$ at $t=0$: $d_1=1$, $d_2=-1$.", "why": "The initial position fixes the constants, just as the initial velocity did."}, {"do": "Substitute $t=2$: $\\mathbf{r}=(8+4+1)\\mathbf{i}+(16-8-1)\\mathbf{j}=13\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}$.", "why": "Substitute the time found in step 3 into the finished position vector."}], "answer": "It moves parallel to $\\mathbf{i}$ when $t=2$ s, at position $(13\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j})$ m.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "ms": 700, "equal": true, "x": [0, 18], "xstep": 2, "y": [-4, 10], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "x / m (i direction)", "ylabel": "y / m (j direction)", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, -1.0], [1.025, -0.9003], [1.05, -0.8013], [1.0751, -0.7028], [1.1001, -0.605], [1.1252, -0.5078], [1.1504, -0.4113], [1.1757, -0.3153], [1.201, -0.22], [1.2264, -0.1253], [1.252, -0.0312], [1.2776, 0.0622], [1.3034, 0.155], [1.3293, 0.2472], [1.3554, 0.3387], [1.3816, 0.4297], [1.408, 0.52], [1.4346, 0.6097], [1.4614, 0.6987], [1.4884, 0.7872], [1.5156, 0.875], [1.5431, 0.9622], [1.5708, 1.0488], [1.5988, 1.1347], [1.627, 1.22], [1.6555, 1.3047], [1.6843, 1.3887], [1.7134, 1.4722], [1.7429, 1.555], [1.7726, 1.6372], [1.8027, 1.7188], [1.8332, 1.7997], [1.864, 1.88], [1.8952, 1.9597], [1.9268, 2.0387], [1.9587, 2.1172], [1.9911, 2.195], [2.0239, 2.2722], [2.0572, 2.3487], [2.0909, 2.4247], [2.125, 2.5], [2.1596, 2.5747], [2.1947, 2.6488], [2.2303, 2.7222], [2.2664, 2.795], [2.303, 2.8672], [2.3401, 2.9387], [2.3778, 3.0097], [2.416, 3.08], [2.4548, 3.1497], [2.4941, 3.2188], [2.5341, 3.2872], [2.5746, 3.355], [2.6158, 3.4222], [2.6575, 3.4888], [2.7, 3.5547], [2.743, 3.62], [2.7867, 3.6847], [2.8311, 3.7487], [2.8761, 3.8122], [2.9219, 3.875], [2.9683, 3.9372], [3.0155, 3.9988], [3.0634, 4.0597], [3.112, 4.12], [3.1614, 4.1797], [3.2115, 4.2387], [3.2624, 4.2972], [3.3141, 4.355], [3.3666, 4.4122], [3.4199, 4.4688], [3.474, 4.5247], [3.529, 4.58], [3.5848, 4.6347], [3.6415, 4.6888], [3.699, 4.7422], [3.7574, 4.795], [3.8167, 4.8472], [3.8769, 4.8987], [3.938, 4.9497], [4.0, 5.0], [4.063, 5.0497], [4.1269, 5.0987], [4.1918, 5.1472], [4.2576, 5.195], [4.3245, 5.2422], [4.3923, 5.2888], [4.4611, 5.3347], [4.531, 5.38], [4.6019, 5.4247], [4.6738, 5.4688], [4.7468, 5.5122], [4.8209, 5.555], [4.896, 5.5972], [4.9722, 5.6387], [5.0496, 5.6797], [5.128, 5.72], [5.2076, 5.7597], [5.2883, 5.7988], [5.3701, 5.8372], [5.4531, 5.875], [5.5373, 5.9122], [5.6227, 5.9487], [5.7092, 5.9847], [5.797, 6.02], [5.886, 6.0547], [5.9762, 6.0887], [6.0677, 6.1222], [6.1604, 6.155], [6.2544, 6.1872], [6.3496, 6.2188], [6.4462, 6.2497], [6.544, 6.28], [6.6432, 6.3097], [6.7436, 6.3388], [6.8455, 6.3672], [6.9486, 6.395], [7.0532, 6.4222], [7.159, 6.4488], [7.2663, 6.4747], [7.375, 6.5], [7.4851, 6.5247], [7.5966, 6.5488], [7.7095, 6.5722], [7.8239, 6.595], [7.9397, 6.6172], [8.057, 6.6387], [8.1757, 6.6597], [8.296, 6.68], [8.4178, 6.6997], [8.541, 6.7188], [8.6658, 6.7372], [8.7921, 6.755], [8.92, 6.7722], [9.0494, 6.7888], [9.1804, 6.8047], [9.313, 6.82], [9.4472, 6.8347], [9.583, 6.8487], [9.7203, 6.8622], [9.8594, 6.875], [10.0, 6.8872], [10.1424, 6.8987], [10.2863, 6.9097], [10.432, 6.92], [10.5793, 6.9297], [10.7284, 6.9387], [10.8791, 6.9472], [11.0316, 6.955], [11.1858, 6.9622], [11.3418, 6.9688], [11.4995, 6.9747], [11.659, 6.98], [11.8203, 6.9847], [11.9833, 6.9887], [12.1482, 6.9922], [12.3149, 6.995], [12.4834, 6.9972], [12.6537, 6.9988], [12.8259, 6.9997], [13.0, 7.0]]}], "points": [{"at": [1, -1], "label": "t = 0"}], "frames": [{"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[1.0, -1.0], [1.5, 1.0]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[1.0, -1.0], [1.0, -2.0]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [1.0, -1.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 0 s, r = (1, −1)", "v = (2) i + (8) j", "a = 0 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[1.516, 0.875], [2.062, 2.625]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[1.516, 0.875], [1.891, -0.125]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [1.5156, 0.875], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 0.25 s, r = (1.52, 0.88)", "v = (2.19) i + (7) j", "a = 1.5 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[2.125, 2.5], [2.812, 4.0]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[2.125, 2.5], [2.875, 1.5]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [2.125, 2.5], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 0.5 s, r = (2.12, 2.5)", "v = (2.75) i + (6) j", "a = 3 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[2.922, 3.875], [3.844, 5.125]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[2.922, 3.875], [4.047, 2.875]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [2.9219, 3.875], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 0.75 s, r = (2.92, 3.88)", "v = (3.69) i + (5) j", "a = 4.5 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[4.0, 5.0], [5.25, 6.0]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[4.0, 5.0], [5.5, 4.0]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [4.0, 5.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 1 s, r = (4, 5)", "v = (5) i + (4) j", "a = 6 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[5.453, 5.875], [7.125, 6.625]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[5.453, 5.875], [7.328, 4.875]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [5.4531, 5.875], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 1.25 s, r = (5.45, 5.88)", "v = (6.69) i + (3) j", "a = 7.5 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[7.375, 6.5], [9.562, 7.0]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[7.375, 6.5], [9.625, 5.5]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [7.375, 6.5], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 1.5 s, r = (7.38, 6.5)", "v = (8.75) i + (2) j", "a = 9 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[9.859, 6.875], [12.656, 7.125]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[9.859, 6.875], [12.484, 5.875]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [9.8594, 6.875], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 1.75 s, r = (9.86, 6.88)", "v = (11.19) i + (1) j", "a = 10.5 i − 4 j"]}, {"lines": [{"c": 1, "pts": [[13.0, 7.0], [16.5, 7.0]], "label": "v (drawn ¼ size)"}, {"c": 2, "pts": [[13.0, 7.0], [16.0, 6.0]], "label": "a (drawn ¼ size)"}], "points": [{"at": [13.0, 7.0], "label": "P"}], "read": ["t = 2 s, r = (13, 7)", "v = (14) i + (0) j", "a = 12 i − 4 j"]}], "ctl": {"type": "play", "label": "s", "vals": ["0", "0.25", "0.5", "0.75", "1", "1.25", "1.5", "1.75", "2"], "start": 8}}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 set the j-component of the velocity equal to zero?", "o": ["The acceleration has no j-component at that moment, so the velocity has none either", "The particle must be at rest when it moves parallel to i, so its speed is zero", "A vector parallel to i has no j-part, so the j-component of velocity must be zero", "The i- and j-components of the velocity must be equal when the direction is along i"], "a": 2, "f": ["The acceleration's j-component is $-4$ at all times, so it is never zero. You have confused acceleration with velocity; the condition is about velocity.", "Parallel to i is not the same as at rest. At $t=2$ the velocity is $14\\mathbf{i}$, so the speed is 14 m s⁻¹, not zero.", "Correct. Moving parallel to i means the velocity is a multiple of i, so its j-component is zero. That gives an equation for $t$.", "Equal components would give motion at 45° to both axes. Motion along i needs the j-component to be zero."]}, "faded": {"task": "A particle has acceleration $(2-6t)\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ m s⁻² and velocity $(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})$ m s⁻¹ at $t=0$. Find its velocity when $t=2$.", "given": ["Integrate: $\\mathbf{v}=(2t-3t^2+c_1)\\mathbf{i}+(3t+c_2)\\mathbf{j}$", "At $t=0$: $c_1=4$ and $c_2=-1$"], "q": "What is the next step, and its result?", "o": ["Put $t=2$ into $\\mathbf{v}$ with $c_1=0$, $c_2=0$: $-8\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$", "Put $t=2$ into $\\mathbf{v}$ with $c_1=4$, $c_2=-1$: $-4\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}$", "Put $t=2$ into $\\mathbf{v}$ with $c_1=4$, $c_2=1$: $-4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}$", "Put $t=2$ into $\\mathbf{v}$ with $c_1=0$, $c_2=-1$: $-8\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}$"], "a": 1, "f": ["This leaves out both constants. $c_1=4$ and $c_2=-1$ are part of $\\mathbf{v}$, so dropping them gives the wrong velocity.", "Correct. $\\mathbf{v}=(2t-3t^2+4)\\mathbf{i}+(3t-1)\\mathbf{j}$. At $t=2$: $(4-12+4)\\mathbf{i}+(6-1)\\mathbf{j}=-4\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}$.", "This takes $c_2=+1$. The initial j-component is $-1$, so the constant is $-1$. Watch the sign.", "This drops $c_1=4$ from the i-component. Both components need their constants."]}, "trap": "Forgetting the constant of integration, or using the same constant for both components. Write $+c_1$ and $+c_2$ every time, then use the initial conditions straight away.", "recap": "Differentiate or integrate each component separately, find every constant from the initial conditions, and use suvat only when acceleration is constant."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-01", "kind": "method", "why": "Indices, surds and expanding appear on every paper as short, mark-rich questions, and every later topic (quadratics, calculus, logarithms) relies on this algebra being accurate.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Laws of indices", "b": "To multiply powers of the same base, **add** the indices: $a^m\\times a^n=a^{m+n}$. To divide, **subtract**: $a^m\\div a^n=a^{m-n}$. For a power of a power, **multiply**: $(a^m)^n=a^{mn}$. A power applies to every factor in a bracket: $(2x^3)^4=16x^{12}$."}, {"h": "Negative and fractional indices", "b": "A **negative index** means a reciprocal: $a^{-n}=\\frac{1}{a^n}$. A **fractional index** means a root: $a^{\\frac1n}=\\sqrt[n]{a}$, and $a^{\\frac mn}=\\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^m$. Take the root first, because it keeps the numbers small. Also $a^0=1$."}, {"h": "Simplifying and rationalising surds", "b": "Use $\\sqrt{ab}=\\sqrt a\\times\\sqrt b$ to simplify: $\\sqrt{48}=\\sqrt{16}\\times\\sqrt3=4\\sqrt3$. To **rationalise** $\\frac{1}{p+\\sqrt q}$, multiply top and bottom by the **conjugate** $p-\\sqrt q$. The denominator becomes $p^2-q$, which is rational."}, {"h": "Expanding brackets", "b": "Multiply **every term** in the first bracket by **every term** in the second, then collect **like terms**. Squaring gives three terms: $(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2$. The same method works with surds, and $(\\sqrt q)^2=q$."}], "example": {"task": "Write $\\frac{(4x^3)^{\\frac32}}{2x^{-1}}$ in the form $kx^n$, where $k$ and $n$ are constants.", "steps": [{"do": "Apply the power to each factor of the bracket: $(4x^3)^{\\frac32}=4^{\\frac32}\\times(x^3)^{\\frac32}$.", "why": "The power acts on the 4 and on the $x^3$, not just on one of them."}, {"do": "Evaluate $4^{\\frac32}=\\left(\\sqrt4\\right)^3=2^3=8$.", "why": "The denominator 2 of the index means a square root. The numerator 3 means cubing."}, {"do": "Simplify $(x^3)^{\\frac32}=x^{3\\times\\frac32}=x^{\\frac92}$.", "why": "A power of a power: multiply the indices."}, {"do": "The numerator is $8x^{\\frac92}$. Divide: $\\frac{8}{2}=4$ and $x^{\\frac92}\\div x^{-1}=x^{\\frac92-(-1)}=x^{\\frac{11}{2}}$.", "why": "Dividing means subtracting indices. Subtracting $-1$ is the same as adding 1."}], "answer": "$4x^{\\frac{11}{2}}$"}, "selfx": {"q": "In the last step, why does $x^{\\frac92}\\div x^{-1}$ give $x^{\\frac{11}{2}}$?", "o": ["Dividing by $x^{-1}$ makes the whole answer negative, so the index changes sign", "The 2 in $2x^{-1}$ is added to the index, giving $\\frac92+2$", "Subtracting the index $-1$ is the same as adding 1, so $\\frac92+1=\\frac{11}{2}$", "The indices are subtracted the other way round, so $-1-\\frac92$ is used"], "a": 2, "f": ["A negative index does not make an expression negative. It only means a reciprocal, and here it changes the subtraction to an addition.", "The 2 is a coefficient and is divided separately. It never combines with an index.", "Correct: $\\frac92-(-1)=\\frac92+1=\\frac{11}{2}$. Dividing by $x^{-1}$ is the same as multiplying by $x$.", "The subtraction is numerator index minus denominator index. Reversing it would give $-\\frac{11}{2}$."]}, "faded": {"task": "Write $\\frac{(9x^4)^{\\frac32}}{3x^{-2}}$ in the form $kx^n$, where $k$ and $n$ are constants.", "given": ["Apply the power to each factor: $(9x^4)^{\\frac32}=9^{\\frac32}\\times(x^4)^{\\frac32}$.", "Evaluate: $9^{\\frac32}=\\left(\\sqrt9\\right)^3=27$ and $(x^4)^{\\frac32}=x^6$, so the numerator is $27x^6$."], "q": "What is the next step, and what is the final answer?", "o": ["$27\\div3=9$ and $x^{6-2}=x^4$, giving $9x^4$", "$27\\div3=9$ and $x^{6-(-2)}=x^8$, giving $9x^8$", "$27\\div3=9$ and $x^{6\\div(-2)}=x^{-3}$, giving $9x^{-3}$", "$27\\div3=9$ and $x^{6\\times(-2)}=x^{-12}$, giving $9x^{-12}$"], "a": 1, "f": ["The denominator index is $-2$, not 2. Subtracting $-2$ means adding 2, so the index is 8, not 4.", "Correct: dividing means subtracting indices, and $6-(-2)=8$. The coefficients divide separately: $27\\div3=9$.", "Dividing powers means subtracting the indices, not dividing them. The index should be $6-(-2)=8$.", "Multiplying indices is for a power of a power. Here you are dividing, so subtract: $6-(-2)=8$."]}, "trap": "Applying a power to only part of a bracket, such as $(2x^3)^4=2x^{12}$. The 2 is raised to the power too: $(2x^3)^4=16x^{12}$. Write out each factor with the power first.", "recap": "Apply every index law to each factor in a bracket, and rationalise a surd denominator by multiplying by its conjugate."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-03", "kind": "method", "why": "Quadratic inequalities and simultaneous equations come up regularly in the pure papers, and the same algebra underpins later work on graphs and the discriminant.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Sketch, then read", "b": "Rearrange so one side is 0, then find the **roots**. Sketch the **parabola** through them. With a positive $x^2$ coefficient it is U-shaped. $>0$ means above the $x$-axis: the two outer regions. $<0$ means below the axis: between the roots."}, {"h": "Writing the answer", "b": "Outside the roots gives two regions joined by **or**: $x<a$ or $x>b$. Between the roots gives one region: $a<x<b$. With $\\le$ or $\\ge$ the roots are **included**. Never write $x<a$ and $x>b$: no number satisfies both."}, {"h": "Negative multipliers", "b": "Multiplying or dividing both sides by a **negative** number **reverses** the inequality sign: $-x>3$ becomes $x<-3$. For a quadratic with a negative $x^2$ term, multiply through by $-1$ first, so you can use the U-shaped sketch."}, {"h": "Simultaneous equations", "b": "Make $y$ the subject of the linear equation and **substitute** it into the quadratic. Solve the quadratic, then put each root back into the **linear** equation to find its $y$. A repeated root means the line is a tangent. If $b^2-4ac<0$, the line and curve do not meet. Drag the slider to move the line on the graph.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [-2, 4], "xstep": 1, "y": [-6, 10], "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[-2.0, 4.0], [-1.9688, 3.876], [-1.9375, 3.7539], [-1.9062, 3.6338], [-1.875, 3.5156], [-1.8438, 3.3994], [-1.8125, 3.2852], [-1.7812, 3.1729], [-1.75, 3.0625], [-1.7188, 2.9541], [-1.6875, 2.8477], [-1.6562, 2.7432], [-1.625, 2.6406], [-1.5938, 2.54], [-1.5625, 2.4414], [-1.5312, 2.3447], [-1.5, 2.25], [-1.4688, 2.1572], [-1.4375, 2.0664], [-1.4062, 1.9775], [-1.375, 1.8906], [-1.3438, 1.8057], [-1.3125, 1.7227], [-1.2812, 1.6416], [-1.25,