 [6.3, 3.555], [6.35, 3.3888], [6.4, 3.22], [6.45, 3.0487], [6.5, 2.875], [6.55, 2.6988], [6.6, 2.52], [6.65, 2.3387], [6.7, 2.155], [6.75, 1.9688], [6.8, 1.78], [6.85, 1.5888], [6.9, 1.395], [6.95, 1.1987], [7.0, 1.0], [7.05, 0.7988], [7.1, 0.595], [7.15, 0.3887], [7.2, 0.18], [7.25, -0.0312], [7.3, -0.245], [7.35, -0.4612], [7.4, -0.68], [7.45, -0.9013], [7.5, -1.125], [7.55, -1.3512], [7.6, -1.58], [7.65, -1.8113], [7.7, -2.045], [7.75, -2.2812], [7.8, -2.52], [7.85, -2.7612], [7.9, -3.005], [7.95, -3.2513], [8.0, -3.5]], "label": "y=2f(x+1)-3"}], "points": [{"at": [4, 6], "label": "(4, 6)", "step": 1}, {"at": [3, 6], "label": "(3, 6)", "step": 2}, {"at": [3, 12], "label": "(3, 12)", "step": 3}, {"at": [3, 9], "label": "(3, 9)", "step": 4}], "steps": ["Start with y = f(x). Its maximum point is (4, 6).", "f(x+1): the bracket acts backwards, so the whole graph moves 1 left. The maximum moves to (3, 6).", "2f(x+1): a vertical stretch with scale factor 2. Every y-coordinate doubles and x stays 3. The maximum is now (3, 12).", "2f(x+1) - 3: the graph moves 3 down. The maximum of y = 2f(x+1) - 3 is (3, 9). The stretch must come before the -3."]}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the $x$-coordinate become 3, not 5, for $f(x+1)$?", "o": ["Adding 1 inside the bracket adds 1 to every $x$-coordinate", "The new graph needs $x+1=4$ to match the old point, so $x=3$", "Maximum points always move left under any transformation", "The 2 in front of the bracket also changes the $x$-coordinate"], "a": 1, "f": ["This is the backwards-bracket slip. Adding inside the bracket moves the graph left, so it subtracts from $x$.", "Right. $f(x+1)$ takes the old value when $x+1=4$, which is $x=3$. The graph shifts 1 left.", "Maximum points do not always move left. The direction depends on the transformation applied.", "A multiplier outside the bracket stretches vertically. It changes $y$ only and never $x$."]}, "faded": {"task": "The curve $y=f(x)$ has a minimum point at $(6,-2)$. Find the minimum point of $y=3f(\\tfrac12 x)+1$.", "given": ["Start: minimum at $(6,-2)$.", "$f(\\tfrac12 x)$: horizontal stretch with scale factor 2, so $x$ doubles: $(12,-2)$."], "q": "Next, apply the 3 in $3f(\\tfrac12 x)$. What is the point now?", "o": ["$(36,-2)$", "$(4,-6)$", "$(12,-6)$", "$(12,1)$"], "a": 2, "f": ["This multiplies $x$ by 3. A multiplier outside the bracket stretches vertically, so it changes $y$ only.", "This divides $x$ by 3, so it mixes up the horizontal changes. The 3 is outside, so it only multiplies $y$.", "Right. The 3 is a vertical stretch with scale factor 3, so $y=-2$ becomes $-6$ and $x$ stays 12. Then $+1$ gives $(12,-5)$.", "This replaces $y$ with the $+1$ and ignores the 3. First multiply $y=-2$ by 3, then add 1 later."]}, "trap": "Treating bracket changes as if they act the way they look. $f(x+3)$ moves left, not right, and $f(2x)$ halves the $x$-coordinates. Check by asking what $x$ gives the original input.", "recap": "Changes inside the bracket alter $x$ in the opposite or reciprocal way, changes outside alter $y$ as written, so apply each in order."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-05", "kind": "method", "why": "Straight-line work appears on all three papers, as short questions and as steps inside longer coordinate geometry problems, so slips here cost marks in many places.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Gradient from two points", "b": "The **gradient** of the line through $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$ is $m=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}$. Subtract in the **same order** on the top and bottom. A positive gradient rises left to right. A negative gradient falls."}, {"h": "Writing the equation", "b": "Given a gradient $m$ and a point $(x_1,y_1)$, use $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$. Alternatively use $y=mx+c$, where $c$ is the **$y$-intercept**. For $ax+by+c=0$, rearrange to $y=\\dots$ first. The **gradient** is then $-\\frac{a}{b}$, not $a$."}, {"h": "Parallel and perpendicular", "b": "**Parallel** lines have equal gradients. For **perpendicular** lines, $m_1m_2=-1$. So the perpendicular gradient is the **negative reciprocal**: flip the fraction and change the sign. The gradient $\\frac23$ becomes $-\\frac32$."}, {"h": "Geometry problems", "b": "Most problems chain these tools. The **midpoint** of $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$ is $\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right)$. A **perpendicular bisector** passes through the midpoint with the perpendicular gradient. Find the gradient first, then the point, then the equation."}], "example": {"task": "The graph shows line L: $3x+5y-20=0$ and the point $(4,-1)$. Find the equation of the line through $(4,-1)$ perpendicular to L, in the form $ax+by+c=0$ with $a$, $b$, $c$ integers.", "steps": [{"do": "Rearrange L: $5y=-3x+20$, so $y=-\\frac35x+4$. The gradient of L is $-\\frac35$.", "why": "The gradient can only be read off once $y$ is on its own with coefficient 1."}, {"do": "Perpendicular gradient $=-1\\div\\left(-\\frac35\\right)=\\frac53$.", "why": "Negative reciprocal: flip $\\frac35$ to $\\frac53$ and change the sign from negative to positive."}, {"do": "Use $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$ with $(4,-1)$: $y+1=\\frac53(x-4)$.", "why": "The new line passes through $(4,-1)$, so that is the point to use."}, {"do": "Multiply by 3: $3y+3=5(x-4)$, so $3y+3=5x-20$.", "why": "This clears the fraction so that the coefficients are integers."}, {"do": "Rearrange to one side: $5x-3y-23=0$.", "why": "This is the form the question asks for. Check: $(4,-1)$ gives $20+3-23=0$."}], "answer": "$5x-3y-23=0$", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-2, 10], "y": [-6, 6], "xstep": 2, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[-2, 5.2], [10, -2]], "label": "L", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, -6], [7, 4]], "label": "perp line", "dash": true, "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [4, -1], "label": "(4,-1)"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "equal": true}}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 rearrange L to $y=\\dots$ before reading off the gradient?", "o": ["The gradient is the number in front of $x$ whatever form the equation is in", "Rearranging turns the gradient into its negative reciprocal", "The gradient is the coefficient of $x$ only when $y$ is alone with coefficient 1", "The equation must be in the form $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$ before you can find $m$"], "a": 2, "f": ["In $3x+5y-20=0$ the 3 is not the gradient. This is the common slip of reading $a$ as the gradient.", "Rearranging does not change the line or its gradient. The negative reciprocal is a separate step, used for perpendicular lines.", "Correct. Only in $y=mx+c$ form is $m$ the coefficient of $x$. Here the gradient is $-\\frac{3}{5}$.", "That form is used after you know $m$ and a point. It is not needed to find the gradient."]}, "faded": {"task": "The graph shows line $L_1$ through $A(1,7)$ and $B(5,1)$, and the point $C(3,2)$. Find the equation of the line through $C$ perpendicular to $L_1$.", "given": ["Gradient of $AB=\\frac{1-7}{5-1}=\\frac{-6}{4}=-\\frac32$."], "q": "What is the gradient of the line through $C$ perpendicular to $AB$?", "o": ["$-\\frac{2}{3}$", "$\\frac{2}{3}$", "$\\frac{3}{2}$", "$-\\frac{3}{2}$"], "a": 1, "f": ["You flipped the fraction but kept the negative sign. A perpendicular gradient needs the sign changed as well.", "Correct. Flip $-\\frac32$ to get $\\frac23$ and change the sign. Check: $-\\frac32\\times\\frac23=-1$.", "You changed the sign but did not flip the fraction. The product $-\\frac32\\times\\frac32$ is not $-1$.", "This is the gradient of $AB$ itself, which would give a parallel line, not a perpendicular one."], "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [0, 8], "y": [0, 8], "xstep": 1, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 7], [5, 1]], "label": "L1", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [1, 7], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5, 1], "label": "B"}, {"at": [3, 2], "label": "C"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "equal": true}}, "trap": "Reading the gradient of $ax+by+c=0$ as $a$. Rearrange to $y=\\dots$ first, so the gradient is $-\\frac{a}{b}$. For a perpendicular line, flip the fraction and change the sign.", "recap": "Find the gradient first, then use $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$; for perpendicular lines use the negative reciprocal."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-06", "kind": "method", "why": "Circle questions almost always combine completing the square with straight-line gradients, so a reliable routine earns most of the marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Reading the equation", "b": "$(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$ has **centre** $(a,b)$ and **radius** $r$. The signs inside the brackets are the opposite of the coordinates. So $(x+1)^2+(y-4)^2=9$ has centre $(-1,4)$ and radius $3$, since $r^2=9$."}, {"h": "Completing the square", "b": "If the equation is written as $x^2+y^2+\\dots=0$, group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms. **Complete the square** on each, then move the constants to the right. The right side is $r^2$, so take the square root to get the **radius**."}, {"h": "Tangents", "b": "A **tangent** touches the circle at one point. It is **perpendicular** to the radius at that point. Find the gradient of the radius from the centre to the point. The tangent gradient is its **negative reciprocal**. Then use $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$."}, {"h": "Chords", "b": "The perpendicular from the centre to a **chord** bisects the chord. So the perpendicular bisector of any chord passes through the **centre**. This gives right-angled triangles: radius as hypotenuse, half-chord and the distance from the centre as the other sides."}], "example": {"task": "Circle $C$ has equation $x^2+y^2-8x+2y-8=0$. Show that $P(7,3)$ lies on $C$, and find the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$.", "steps": [{"do": "Complete the square: $(x-4)^2-16+(y+1)^2-1-8=0$.", "why": "Halve each linear coefficient and subtract its square to compensate."}, {"do": "Rearrange to $(x-4)^2+(y+1)^2=25$. Centre $(4,-1)$, radius $5$.", "why": "The constants move across: $16+1+8=25$. Brackets give the centre with signs flipped, and $r=\\sqrt{25}$."}, {"do": "Substitute $P$: $(7-4)^2+(3+1)^2=9+16=25$.", "why": "The left side equals $r^2$, so $P$ lies on the circle."}, {"do": "Gradient of radius $CP=\\dfrac{3-(-1)}{7-4}=\\dfrac43$. Tangent gradient is $-\\dfrac34$.", "why": "The tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so use the negative reciprocal."}, {"do": "$y-3=-\\dfrac34(x-7)$, so $4y-12=-3x+21$, giving $3x+4y-33=0$.", "why": "Use the tangent gradient and the point of contact, then clear the fraction."}], "answer": "$P$ lies on $C$ because $3^2+4^2=25$. The tangent at $P$ is $3x+4y-33=0$."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the tangent gradient $-\\frac34$ and not $\\frac43$?", "o": ["The tangent is parallel to the radius, so it has the same gradient with the sign changed", "Gradients on the right-hand side of a circle are always negative", "The tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal", "The tangent passes through the centre, so its gradient comes from the centre's coordinates"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong: a tangent is perpendicular to the radius, not parallel. Changing only the sign ignores the reciprocal.", "Wrong: no such rule exists. The sign comes from the perpendicularity of tangent and radius, not from the position on the circle.", "Right: the tangent meets the radius at $90^\\circ$, so the two gradients multiply to $-1$.", "Wrong: a tangent touches the circle at one point and never passes through the centre. The centre is only used to find the radius gradient."]}, "faded": {"task": "Find the centre and radius of the circle $x^2+y^2+6x-2y-3=0$.", "given": ["Group terms: $(x^2+6x)+(y^2-2y)=3$", "Complete the square: $(x+3)^2-9+(y-1)^2-1=3$"], "q": "What is the next step, and the final result?", "o": ["Add $10$ to both sides: centre $(3,-1)$, radius $\\sqrt{13}$", "Add $10$ to both sides: centre $(-3,1)$, radius $\\sqrt{13}$", "Add $10$ to both sides: centre $(-3,1)$, radius $13$", "Add $9$ only to both sides: centre $(-3,1)$, radius $\\sqrt{12}$"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong: $3+9+1=13$ is right, but you copied the signs from the brackets. $(x+3)$ gives $a=-3$, and $(y-1)$ gives $b=1$.", "Right: moving $-9$ and $-1$ across gives $(x+3)^2+(y-1)^2=13$. Centre $(-3,1)$ and $r=\\sqrt{13}$.", "Wrong: the centre is right, but $13$ is $r^2$. The radius is the square root, $\\sqrt{13}$.", "Wrong: both $-9$ and $-1$ must be moved across, so you add $10$, not $9$. The right side is $3+9+1=13$."]}, "trap": "Copying signs from the brackets: $(y+1)^2$ gives $b=-1$. Also quoting $r^2$ as the radius. After completing the square, always write the centre and then $r=\\sqrt{\\text{right side}}$.", "recap": "Complete the square to reach $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$; a tangent's gradient is the negative reciprocal of the radius gradient at that point."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-07", "kind": "method", "why": "Factorising cubics and proving statements come up often on AQA papers, usually as a multi-mark 'show that… hence' question or a 'prove that' question.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The factor theorem", "b": "$(x-a)$ is a **factor** of $f(x)$ exactly when $f(a)=0$. For $(x+1)$, $a=-1$, so test $f(-1)$. For $(2x-1)$, test $f(\\tfrac12)$, the value that makes the bracket **zero**."}, {"h": "Algebraic division", "b": "Once $f(a)=0$, write $f(x)=(x-a)(\\text{quadratic})$. Find the quadratic by **comparing coefficients** or by **long division**. Match the $x^3$ and constant terms first, then $x^2$, and use the $x$ term as a check."}, {"h": "Deduction and exhaustion", "b": "**Proof by deduction** starts from known facts and uses algebra, such as writing an even number as $2n$. **Proof by exhaustion** splits the claim into a finite number of cases and checks every one, such as $n$ even and $n$ odd."}, {"h": "Counterexamples", "b": "To disprove a claim about all cases, find one **counterexample**: a case that meets the conditions but breaks the claim. Check it really meets them. Examples that work, however many, are never a **proof**."}], "example": {"task": "Show that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=x^3+2x^2-5x-6$, then factorise $f(x)$ fully.", "steps": [{"do": "Since $x+1=x-(-1)$, work out $f(-1)$.", "why": "The factor theorem needs $a=-1$ here."}, {"do": "$f(-1)=-1+2+5-6=0$, so $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$.", "why": "A zero value is exactly the condition for a factor."}, {"do": "Write $x^3+2x^2-5x-6=(x+1)(x^2+bx+c)$. The constant terms give $c=-6$.", "why": "The constant term of the product is $1\\times c$."}, {"do": "Compare $x^2$ terms: $b+1=2$, so $b=1$. Check the $x$ term: $c+b=-5$ ✓.", "why": "$x^2$ comes from $x\\cdot bx$ and $1\\cdot x^2$. The check catches slips."}, {"do": "Factorise $x^2+x-6=(x+3)(x-2)$.", "why": "The question asks for a full factorisation, so the quadratic must be split too."}], "answer": "$f(-1)=0$, so $(x+1)$ is a factor. $f(x)=(x+1)(x^2+x-6)=(x+1)(x+3)(x-2)$."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 test $f(-1)$ and not $f(1)$?", "o": ["Because the number in the bracket is $+1$, and the factor theorem tests that number", "Because $-1$ is a factor of the constant $-6$, and $f(1)$ cannot be zero", "Because $x+1=x-(-1)$, so the factor theorem needs $a=-1$", "Because $f(1)$ is never zero for a cubic whose constant term is negative"], "a": 2, "f": ["The theorem tests $a$ in $(x-a)$. The bracket has $+1$, so $a=-1$. Using the bracket's sign is the classic sign slip.", "$1$ is also a factor of $-6$, and $f(1)$ could be zero for some cubics. Being a factor of the constant does not decide the test.", "Right: $(x+1)$ is $(x-a)$ with $a=-1$, so $(x+1)$ is a factor exactly when $f(-1)=0$.", "Not true: $x^3-1$ has a negative constant and $f(1)=0$. The sign of the constant does not decide which value to test."]}, "faded": {"task": "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)=x^3-x^2-10x-8$, find the quadratic factor.", "given": ["$f(4)=64-16-40-8=0$, so $(x-4)$ is a factor.", "Write $f(x)=(x-4)(x^2+bx+c)$."], "q": "Compare coefficients. Which values of $b$ and $c$ are correct?", "o": ["$b=-5,\\ c=2$", "$b=3,\\ c=2$", "$b=3,\\ c=-2$", "$b=-1,\\ c=-8$"], "a": 1, "f": ["You wrote $b+4=-1$. The $x^2$ terms are $bx^2$ and $-4x^2$, so $b-4=-1$ and $b=3$.", "Right: $b-4=-1$ gives $b=3$, and $-4c=-8$ gives $c=2$. Check the $x$ term: $c-4b=2-12=-10$ ✓.", "$b$ is right, but the constant term is $-4c=-8$, so $c=2$. You slipped on the sign when dividing.", "You copied the cubic's coefficients. They do not carry over. Compare terms after expanding $(x-4)(x^2+bx+c)$."]}, "trap": "Testing $f(1)$ for the factor $(x+1)$; use $f(-1)$. Also, several cases that work are never a proof. Prove with algebra or check every case; one counterexample disproves.", "recap": "$(x-a)$ is a factor exactly when $f(a)=0$; one counterexample disproves a claim, but examples that work never prove it."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-08", "kind": "method", "why": "Binomial expansion is a standard AS and A-level calculation, often worth 4 to 5 marks, and it returns in Year 13 with fractional powers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Binomial coefficients", "b": "The **binomial coefficient** is $\\binom{n}{r}=\\dfrac{n!}{r!\\,(n-r)!}$, read \"n choose r\". Here $n!=n\\times(n-1)\\times\\dots\\times1$. It is symmetrical: $\\binom{n}{r}=\\binom{n}{n-r}$. These are the numbers in row $n$ of **Pascal's triangle**. Your calculator has an nCr key."}, {"h": "The general term", "b": "$(a+b)^n=\\sum\\binom{n}{r}a^{n-r}b^r$, for $r=0,1,\\dots,n$. The **power of $a$** falls from $n$ to 0 as the **power of $b$** rises from 0 to $n$. In every term the two powers add to $n$. There are $n+1$ terms."}, {"h": "Brackets like (p + qx)ⁿ", "b": "Treat $qx$ as the whole of $b$. The term in $x^r$ is $\\binom{n}{r}p^{n-r}q^r x^r$. Put **brackets** round $qx$ before applying the power, so $q$ is raised to the power too. If $q$ is negative, keep its **sign** inside the bracket."}, {"h": "An extra bracket", "b": "For something like $(1+x)(2+x)^5$, expand only as many terms as you need. Multiply each term of the first bracket by the terms of the expansion. Then **collect like terms**. The $x^2$ term gets **contributions** from every pairing whose powers add to 2."}], "example": {"task": "Find the first three terms, in ascending powers of $x$, of the expansion of $(2+3x)^5$.", "steps": [{"do": "Write the general term: $\\binom{5}{r}\\,2^{5-r}\\,(3x)^r$.", "why": "Here $a=2$, $b=3x$ and $n=5$. The whole of $3x$ goes in the bracket."}, {"do": "For $r=0$: $\\binom50\\,2^5=1\\times32=32$.", "why": "$\\binom50=1$ and $(3x)^0=1$, so the constant term is just $2^5$."}, {"do": "For $r=1$: $\\binom51\\,2^4\\,(3x)=5\\times16\\times3x=240x$.", "why": "$\\binom51=5$. Multiply the coefficients 5, 16 and 3."}, {"do": "For $r=2$: $\\binom52\\,2^3\\,(3x)^2=10\\times8\\times9x^2=720x^2$.", "why": "$\\binom52=\\frac{5!}{2!\\,3!}=10$. Squaring $3x$ gives $9x^2$, not $3x^2$."}, {"do": "Write the terms in ascending powers of $x$.", "why": "The question asks for the first three terms, so stop after $r=2$."}], "answer": "$(2+3x)^5=32+240x+720x^2+\\dots$"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the $r=2$ term use $(3x)^2=9x^2$ rather than $3x^2$?", "o": ["Only $x$ is raised to the power $r$, and 3 is just a multiplier", "The power applies to the whole of $b=3x$, so the 3 is squared as well", "Squaring $3x$ means doubling the 3, which gives $6x^2$", "The 3 is already included in the binomial coefficient 10"], "a": 1, "f": ["This is the slip the trap warns about. $b$ is the whole of $3x$, so the power $r$ applies to the 3 too.", "Right. $b=3x$, so $b^2=(3x)^2=9x^2$. The coefficient is raised to the power as well as $x$.", "Squaring means multiplying by itself, not doubling. $(3x)^2=3x\\times3x=9x^2$.", "$\\binom52=10$ depends only on $n$ and $r$. It never contains the 3 from $3x$."]}, "faded": {"task": "Find the first three terms, in ascending powers of $x$, of $(3-2x)^4$.", "given": ["General term: $\\binom4r\\,3^{4-r}\\,(-2x)^r$", "$r=0$: $\\binom40\\,3^4=81$"], "q": "What is the $r=1$ term?", "o": ["$216x$", "$-108x$", "$-216x$", "$-72x$"], "a": 2, "f": ["The magnitude is right but the sign is lost. $(-2x)^1$ is negative, so the term is negative.", "Here $-2$ has been replaced by $-1$. The full $-2$ must be used: $4\\times27\\times(-2x)$.", "Right. $\\binom41\\,3^3\\,(-2x)=4\\times27\\times(-2x)=-216x$.", "The power of 3 is wrong. For $r=1$ it is $3^{4-1}=3^3=27$, not $3^2$."]}, "trap": "Raising only $x$ to the power and forgetting the coefficient, or dropping a minus sign. Write $(qx)^r$ in brackets first, as in $(-2x)^2=4x^2$, then multiply by $\\binom{n}{r}p^{n-r}$.", "recap": "The $x^r$ term of $(p+qx)^n$ is $\\binom{n}{r}p^{n-r}q^r x^r$, with $qx$ bracketed so its sign and coefficient are raised to the power."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-09", "kind": "method", "why": "Triangle problems using the sine rule, cosine rule, area formula and exact trig values come up regularly in Paper 1 and Paper 2, often as multi-mark questions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Choosing the right rule", "b": "Use the **sine rule**, $\\frac{a}{\\sin A}=\\frac{b}{\\sin B}$, only when you know a side and its **opposite angle** as a pair. Use the **cosine rule** when you know two sides and the angle between them, or all three sides. Finding an angle by the sine rule can give two answers: $\\theta$ and $180^\\circ-\\theta$."}, {"h": "Cosine rule and area", "b": "**Cosine rule**: $a^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\\cos A$. To find an angle: $\\cos A=\\frac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2bc}$. **Area** $=\\frac12ab\\sin C$, where $C$ is the **included angle** between sides $a$ and $b$. A negative cosine means the angle is obtuse."}, {"h": "Exact values and graphs", "b": "Learn $\\sin30^\\circ=\\frac12$, $\\cos30^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, $\\tan30^\\circ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$; at $45^\\circ$: $\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2},1$; at $60^\\circ$: $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\frac12,\\sqrt3$. Press play to watch the graph draw: sin and cos have **period** $360^\\circ$ and lie between $-1$ and $1$. Tan has period $180^\\circ$ with **asymptotes** at $90^\\circ$, $270^\\circ$ and every $180^\\circ$ from these.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "draw", "pen": true, "read": ["x (°)", "y"], "dp": 2, "ms": 7000, "x": [0, 360], "xstep": 90, "y": [-2, 2], "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "x / degrees", "ylabel": "y", "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "lines": [{"dash": true, "c": 3, "label": "asymptotes", "pts": [[90, -2], [90, 2], [270, 2], [270, -2]]}, {"c": 0, "step": 1, "pts": [[0.0, 0.0], [2.25, 0.0393], [4.5, 0.0785], [6.75, 0.1175], [9.0, 0.1564], [11.25, 0.1951], [13.5, 0.2334], [15.75, 0.2714], [18.0, 0.309], [20.25, 0.3461], [22.5, 0.3827], [24.75, 0.4187], [27.0, 0.454], [29.25, 0.4886], [31.5, 0.5225], [33.75, 0.5556], [36.0, 0.5878], [38.25, 0.6191], 