37, -0.3762], [339.7801, -0.3683], [340.1766, -0.3605], [340.5731, -0.3527], [340.9695, -0.3449], [341.366, -0.3372], [341.7625, -0.3295], [342.1589, -0.3219], [342.5554, -0.3142], [342.9519, -0.3066], [343.3483, -0.2991], [343.7448, -0.2916], [344.1413, -0.2841], [344.5377, -0.2766], [344.9342, -0.2692], [345.3307, -0.2618], [345.7271, -0.2544], [346.1236, -0.247], [346.5201, -0.2397], [346.9166, -0.2324], [347.313, -0.2251], [347.7095, -0.2179], [348.106, -0.2106], [348.5024, -0.2034], [348.8989, -0.1962], [349.2954, -0.189], [349.6918, -0.1819], [350.0883, -0.1747], [350.4848, -0.1676], [350.8812, -0.1605], [351.2777, -0.1534], [351.6742, -0.1463], [352.0706, -0.1393], [352.4671, -0.1322], [352.8636, -0.1252], [353.26, -0.1182], [353.6565, -0.1112], [354.053, -0.1042], [354.4494, -0.0972], [354.8459, -0.0902], [355.2424, -0.0832], [355.6389, -0.0763], [356.0353, -0.0693], [356.4318, -0.0624], [356.8283, -0.0554], [357.2247, -0.0485], [357.6212, -0.0415], [358.0177, -0.0346], [358.4141, -0.0277], [358.8106, -0.0208], [359.2071, -0.0138], [359.6035, -0.0069], [360.0, -0.0]]}], "cap": "sin and cos repeat every 360° and stay between −1 and 1. tan repeats every 180°, with asymptotes at 90° and 270°."}}], "example": {"task": "In triangle $ABC$, $AB=7$ cm, $BC=9$ cm and $AC=12$ cm. Find angle $B$ to 1 d.p. and the area of the triangle to 3 s.f.", "steps": [{"do": "Three sides, no angle, so use the cosine rule for $B$, opposite $AC$: $\\cos B=\\frac{9^2+7^2-12^2}{2\\times9\\times7}$.", "why": "No side-and-opposite-angle pair is known, so the sine rule cannot start. The side opposite $B$ is the one subtracted."}, {"do": "$\\cos B=\\frac{81+49-144}{126}=\\frac{-14}{126}=-\\frac19$.", "why": "Work out the numerator carefully. The negative result is allowed and tells you something."}, {"do": "$B=\\cos^{-1}\\left(-\\frac19\\right)=96.4^\\circ$ (1 d.p.).", "why": "A negative cosine gives an obtuse angle, which fits the longest side being opposite $B$."}, {"do": "$B$ lies between sides $BA=7$ and $BC=9$, so area $=\\frac12\\times9\\times7\\times\\sin B$.", "why": "The area formula needs the angle between the two sides used."}, {"do": "$\\sin B=\\frac{\\sqrt{80}}{9}=\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{9}$, so area $=31.5\\times\\frac{4\\sqrt5}{9}=14\\sqrt5\\approx31.3$ cm².", "why": "Use $\\sin^2B=1-\\cos^2B$, or a calculator, with the unrounded angle."}], "answer": "$B=96.4^\\circ$ and the area is $14\\sqrt5\\approx31.3$ cm²."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 use the cosine rule rather than the sine rule?", "o": ["The cosine rule is always more accurate than the sine rule", "No side is given with its opposite angle, so the sine rule cannot be started", "The sine rule only works in right-angled triangles", "The triangle has an obtuse angle, so the sine rule is not allowed"], "a": 1, "f": ["Accuracy is not the reason. Both rules are exact. You choose by which information is given.", "Right. The sine rule needs a known side and opposite angle pair, and with three sides alone there is none.", "The sine rule works in any triangle, not only right-angled ones. The real issue is the missing side and angle pair.", "Obtuse angles are fine for the sine rule, though they can cause ambiguity. The reason here is the missing pair."], "f_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "In triangle $ABC$, $AB=6$, $BC=7$ and $CA=9$ cm. Find angle $C$, opposite $AB$.", "given": ["Three sides are known, so use the cosine rule for the angle.", "Label $a=BC=7$, $b=CA=9$, $c=AB=6$, so $\\cos C=\\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2ab}$."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$\\cos C=\\dfrac{49+81-36}{7\\times9}=\\dfrac{94}{63}$", "$\\cos C=\\dfrac{49+81+36}{2\\times7\\times9}=\\dfrac{166}{126}$", "$\\cos C=\\dfrac{49+81-36}{2\\times7\\times9}=\\dfrac{94}{126}$", "$\\cos C=\\dfrac{36+81-49}{2\\times6\\times9}=\\dfrac{68}{108}$"], "a": 2, "f": ["You dropped the 2 in the denominator. This gives a cosine above 1, which is impossible, so check the formula.", "The sign is wrong: $c^2$ must be subtracted, not added. This also gives a cosine above 1.", "Correct. Substitute into $\\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2ab}$ to get $\\frac{94}{126}=\\frac{47}{63}$, then take $\\cos^{-1}$.", "This is the formula for angle $A$, opposite $BC$. You mixed up which side is opposite the angle you want."], "f_note": null}, "trap": "Using $\\frac12ab\\sin C$ with an angle that is not between the two sides, or using the sine rule with no side and opposite angle pair. Check what is given first.", "recap": "Match the rule to the information: sine rule for a side and opposite angle pair, otherwise cosine rule, and area needs the included angle."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p1-10", "kind": "method", "why": "Solving trig equations is a regular multi-mark pure question, and these two identities turn awkward equations into quadratics you can already solve.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The two identities", "b": "**$\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$** and **$\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$** are true for every angle (tan only where $\\cos x\\ne0$). Rearranged, $\\sin^2x=1-\\cos^2x$ and $\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x$. Use them to rewrite one expression in terms of another."}, {"h": "Get one function only", "b": "If an equation mixes $\\sin^2x$ and $\\cos x$, replace the squared term so that **one trig function** remains. If it contains $\\tan x$, replace it with $\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$. To **prove an identity**, work on one side only until it matches the other."}, {"h": "Factorise, never divide", "b": "Collect everything on one side, giving a **quadratic** in $\\sin x$ or $\\cos x$, and factorise. Set each factor to zero. Do not divide by $\\sin x$ or $\\cos x$: a common factor can be zero, so dividing **loses solutions**."}, {"h": "Find every solution", "b": "Solve each factor, then use **symmetry**. For $\\sin$: $x$ and $180^\\circ-x$. For $\\cos$: $x$ and $360^\\circ-x$. For $\\tan$: $x$ and $x+180^\\circ$. Add or subtract $360^\\circ$ to bring angles into the **range**. Reject any value of $\\sin x$ or $\\cos x$ outside $-1$ to $1$. Drag the slider on the graph to see the cos pair.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "x": [-1.5, 1.5], "xstep": 0.5, "y": [-1.5, 1.5], "ystep": 0.5, "equal": true, "xlabel": "x = cos angle", "ylabel": "y = sin angle", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.9992, 0.0393], [0.9969, 0.0785], [0.9931, 0.1175], [0.9877, 0.1564], [0.9808, 0.1951], [0.9724, 0.2334], [0.9625, 0.2714], [0.9511, 0.309], [0.9382, 0.3461], [0.9239, 0.3827], [0.9081, 0.4187], [0.891, 0.454], [0.8725, 0.4886], [0.8526, 0.5225], [0.8315, 0.5556], [0.809, 0.5878], [0.7853, 0.6191], [0.7604, 0.6494], [0.7343, 0.6788], [0