, [75, 3.864]], "label": "sec x", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[105, -3.864], [110, -2.924], [120, -2], [135, -1.414], [150, -1.155], [180, -1], [210, -1.155], [225, -1.414], [240, -2], [250, -2.924], [255, -3.864]], "c": 2}, {"pts": [[285, 3.864], [290, 2.924], [300, 2], [315, 1.414], [330, 1.155], [360, 1]], "c": 3}, {"pts": [[90, -4], [90, 4]], "dash": true, "label": "asymptote", "c": 4}, {"pts": [[270, -4], [270, 4]], "dash": true, "c": 5}], "points": []}}, {"h": "Two new identities", "b": "Divide $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$ by $\\cos^2x$ to get **$1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$**. Divide by $\\sin^2x$ to get **$1+\\cot^2x=\\operatorname{cosec}^2x$**. Use them to swap $\\tan^2x$ or $\\cot^2x$ for a squared reciprocal function."}, {"h": "Inverse trig functions", "b": "$\\arcsin x$ has **range** $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le y\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. $\\arccos x$ has range $0\\le y\\le\\pi$. $\\arctan x$ has range $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}<y<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Each graph is the restricted original graph reflected in $y=x$, as the graph shows for arcsin. A calculator gives only these **principal values**.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "x": [-2, 2], "y": [-2, 2], "xstep": 0.5, "ystep": 0.5, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y", "equal": true, "lines": [{"pts": [[-1.5708, -1], [-1.4, -0.985], [-1.2, -0.932], [-1, -0.841], [-0.8, -0.717], [-0.6, -0.565], [-0.4, -0.389], [-0.2, -0.199], [0, 0], [0.2, 0.199], [0.4, 0.389], [0.6, 0.565], [0.8, 0.717], [1, 0.841], [1.2, 0.932], [1.4, 0.985], [1.5708, 1]], "label": "sin x", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[-1, -1.5708], [-0.985, -1.4], [-0.932, -1.2], [-0.841, -1], [-0.717, -0.8], [-0.565, -0.6], [-0.389, -0.4], [-0.199, -0.2], [0, 0], [0.199, 0.2], [0.389, 0.4], [0.565, 0.6], [0.717, 0.8], [0.841, 1], [0.932, 1.2], [0.985, 1.4], [1, 1.5708]], "label": "arcsin x", "c": 1}, {"dash": true, "pts": [[-2.0, -2.0], [-1.975, -1.975], [-1.95, -1.95], [-1.925, -1.925], [-1.9, -1.9], [-1.875, -1.875], [-1.85, -1.85], [-1.825, -1.825], [-1.8, -1.8], [-1.775, -1.775], [-1.75, -1.75], [-1.725, -1.725], [-1