75, 0.682656], [-0.5, 0.8125], [-0.425, 0.945156], [-0.35, 1.080625], [-0.275, 1.218906], [-0.2, 1.36], [-0.125, 1.503906], [-0.05, 1.650625], [0.025, 1.800156], [0.1, 1.9525], [0.175, 2.107656], [0.25, 2.265625], [0.325, 2.426406], [0.4, 2.59], [0.475, 2.756406], [0.55, 2.925625], [0.625, 3.097656], [0.7, 3.2725], [0.775, 3.450156], [0.85, 3.630625], [0.925, 3.813906], [1.0, 4.0], [1.075, 4.188906], [1.15, 4.380625], [1.225, 4.575156], [1.3, 4.7725], [1.375, 4.972656], [1.45, 5.175625], [1.525, 5.381406], [1.6, 5.59], [1.675, 5.801406], [1.75, 6.015625], [1.825, 6.232656], [1.9, 6.4525], [1.975, 6.675156], [2.05, 6.900625], [2.125, 7.128906], [2.2, 7.36], [2.275, 7.593906], [2.35, 7.830625], [2.425, 8.070156], [2.5, 8.3125], [2.575, 8.557656], [2.65, 8.805625], [2.725, 9.056406], [2.8, 9.31], [2.875, 9.566406], [2.95, 9.825625], [3.025, 10.087656], [3.1, 10.3525], [3.175, 10.620156], [3.25, 10.890625], [3.325, 11.163906], [3.4, 11.44], [3.475, 11.718906], [3.55, 12.000625], [3.625, 12.285156], [3.7, 12.5725], [3.775, 12.862656], [3.85, 13.155625], [3.925, 13.451406], [4.0, 13.75]], "c": 0, "label": "curve"}, {"pts": [[-1, -1], [3, 9]], "label": "tangent", "dash": true, "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [1, 4], "label": "t = 1"}], "xnums": true, "ynums": true}}, "selfx": {"q": "In step 5, why is $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ equal to $\\frac{2t+3}{2}$ and not just $2t+3$?", "o": ["Because you divide $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ by $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ to get change per unit of $x$", "Because you subtract $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ from $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ to find the gradient", "Because you divide $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ by $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ to get change per unit of $x$", "Because you must substitute $t=1$ first, which makes the denominator $2$ cancel"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ measures change in $y$ per unit $t$. Dividing by $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ converts this to change in $y$ per unit $x$, which is the gradient.", "Wrong. Subtracting is not part of the rule. The two derivatives are divided, by the chain rule.", "Wrong. This inverts the fraction. The gradient is $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ divided by $\\frac{dx}{dt}$, not the other way round.", "Wrong. Substituting $t$ first does not change the rule. The $2$ comes from $\\frac{dx}{dt}$, and it is always divided by."]}, "faded": {"task": "A curve has $x=3t+2$, $y=2t^2-1$. Find $y$ in terms of $x$.", "given": ["Rearrange $x=3t+2$ to make $t$ the subject.", "$t=\\frac{x-2}{3}$"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["$y=\\frac{2(x-2)^2}{3}-1$", "$y=2\\left(\\frac{x+2}{3}\\right)^2-1$", "$y=2\\left(\\frac{x-2}{3}\\right)^2-1$", "$y=\\frac{2(x-2)}{9}-1$"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Only the numerator has been squared. The denominator $3$ is part of $t$, so it must be squared too.", "Wrong. The sign is wrong. From $x=3t+2$ you subtract $2$, so $t=\\frac{x-2}{3}$.", "Right. Replace $t$ in $y$ by $\\frac{x-2}{3}$, keeping the square on the whole bracket. Expanding comes after.", "Wrong. This squares the denominator but not the numerator $x-2$. Substitute exactly, keeping $t^2$ as a squared bracket."]}, "trap": "Giving $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ as the gradient. Always write $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$ first. When substituting for $t$, square the whole bracket, including any denominator.", "recap": "To convert, eliminate $t$ by substitution or an identity. For gradient, divide $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ by $\\frac{dx}{dt}$."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "p2-09", "kind": "method", "why": "Pure questions on all three papers use these rules, usually as the first step towards a gradient, a stationary point or a point of inflection.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Standard derivatives", "b": "$\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{kx})=ke^{kx}$, $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x)=\\frac1x$, $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin kx)=k\\cos kx$, $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos kx)=-k\\sin kx$, $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan kx)=k\\sec^2 kx$. These are **standard results**. The trig results only hold when $x$ is in **radians**."}, {"h": "Chain rule", "b": "For a function of a function, $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy}{du}\\times\\frac{du}{dx}$. Differentiate the **outer** function, keep the inside unchanged, then multiply by the derivative of the **inner** function. For $\\ln f(x)$ this gives $\\frac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$."}, {"h": "Product rule", "b": "If $y=uv$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}=u\\frac{dv}{dx}+v\\frac{du}{dx}$. Use the **product rule** when two different functions of $x$ are multiplied. Never multiply the two derivatives together. Afterwards, **factorise** common factors such as $e^{kx}$."}, {"h": "Quotient rule", "b": "If $y=\\frac{u}{v}$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{v\\frac{du}{dx}-u\\frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2}$. The order matters: **denominator times derivative of the top**, minus top times derivative of the bottom. A fraction is zero only when its **numerator** is zero."}], "example": {"task": "Find the exact coordinates of the stationary point of the curve $y=\\frac{\\ln x}{x^2}$, for $x>0$. Press Play on the graph and watch where the gradient dy/dx crosses zero.", "steps": [{"do": "Let $u=\\ln x$ and $v=x^2$. Then $u'=\\frac1x$ and $v'=2x$.", "why": "The curve is a fraction of two different functions, so use the quotient rule."}, {"do": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2\\cdot\\frac1x-\\ln x\\cdot 2x}{(x^2)^2}=\\frac{x-2x\\ln x}{x^4}$", "why": "Substitute into $\\frac{vu'-uv'}{v^2}$ in the correct order. Note $x^2\\cdot\\frac1x=x$."}, {"do": "Factorise and cancel: $\\frac{x(1-2\\ln x)}{x^4}=\\frac{1-2\\ln x}{x^3}$.", "why": "A simpler fraction makes the next step easier."}, {"do": "Set $\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$, so $1-2\\ln x=0$, giving $\\ln x=\\frac12$ and $x=e^{1/2}=\\sqrt e$.", "why": "Stationary points have zero gradient. Only the numerator can make the fraction zero."}, {"do": "Find $y$: $y=\\frac{\\ln\\sqrt e}{(\\sqrt e)^2}=\\frac{1/2}{e}=\\frac{1}{2e}$.", "why": "The question asks for coordinates, so substitute $x$ back into the original curve, not the derivative."}], "answer": "The stationary point is $\\left(\\sqrt e,\\ \\frac{1}{2e}\\right)$.", "fig": {"type": "graph", "anim": "frames", "ms": 250, "x": [0, 4], "xstep": 0.5, "y": [-0.5, 1.25], "ystep": 0.25, "xlabel": "x", "ylabel": "y and dy/dx", "lines": [{"c": 0, "pts": [[1.0, 0.0], [1.0188, 0.0179], [1.0375, 0.0342], [1.0562, 0.0491], [1.075, 0.0626], [1.0938, 0.0749], [1.1125, 0.0861], [1.1313, 0.0964], [1.15, 0.1057], [1.1687, 0.1142], [1.1875, 0.1219], [1.2063, 0.1289], [1.225, 0.1352], [1.2437, 0.141], [1.2625, 0.1462], [1.2812, 0.151], [1.3, 0.1552], [1.3188, 0.1591], [1.3375, 0.1626], [1.3562, 0.1657], [1.375, 0.1684], [1.3938, 0.1709], [1.4125, 0.1731], [1.4312, 0.175], [1.45, 0.1767], [1.4688, 0.1782], [1.4875, 0.1795], [1.5063, 0.1805], [1.525, 0.1815], [1.5437, 0.1822], [1.5625, 0.1828], [1.5813, 0.1833], [1.6, 0.1836], [1.6187, 0.1838], [1.6375, 0.1839], [1.6562, 0.1839], [1.675, 0.1838], [1.6938, 0.1837], [1.7125, 0.1834], [1.7312, 0.1831], [1.75, 0.1827], [1.7688, 0.1823], [1.7875, 0.1818], [1.8062, 0.1812], [1.825, 0.1806], [1.8438, 0.18], [1.8625, 0.1793], [1.8813, 0.1786], [1.9, 0.1778], [1.9187, 0.177], [1.9375, 0.1762], [1.9563, 0.1753], [1.975, 0.1745], [1.9937, 0.1736], [2.0125, 0.1727], [2.0312, 0.1718], [2.05, 0.1708], [2.0688, 0.1699], [2.0875, 0.1689], [2.1063, 0.1679], [2.125, 0.1669], [2.1437, 0.1659], [2.1625, 0.1649], [2.1812, 0.1639], [2.2, 0.1629], [2.2188, 0.1619], [2.2375, 0.1609], [2.2563, 0.1598], [2.275, 0.1588], [2.2938, 0.1578], [2.3125, 0.1568], [2.3312