tion mean: $H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0$. Then $H_1$ is $\\mu<\\mu_0$ or $\\mu>\\mu_0$ (**one-tailed**), or $\\mu\\neq\\mu_0$ (**two-tailed**). 'Changed' or 'different' means two-tailed. 'Lower' or 'increased' means one-tailed. Assume $H_0$ is true while you calculate."}, {"h": "Two ways to decide", "b": "**p-value** method: find the probability of a sample mean at least as extreme as yours, doubling it if two-tailed, and compare it with the **significance level**. Critical value method: find the **critical region**. At 5%, $z=1.645$ or $-1.645$ (one-tailed) or $z=\\pm1.96$ (two-tailed). Both give the same decision."}, {"h": "Writing the conclusion", "b": "If the p-value is less than or equal to the significance level, **reject** $H_0$. Otherwise **do not reject** it. Then answer in context: 'There is (not) sufficient evidence that…'. Never say $H_0$ is proved true or false. A test only gives evidence."}], "example": {"task": "Bag masses are Normally distributed with mean 500 g and standard deviation 8 g. After servicing, a random sample of 16 bags has mean 504.5 g. Test at 5% whether the mean has changed, assuming $\\sigma$ is unchanged.", "steps": [{"do": "$H_0:\\mu=500$, $H_1:\\mu\\neq500$, where $\\mu$ is the population mean mass of a bag.", "why": "'Changed' allows either direction, so the test is two-tailed."}, {"do": "Under $H_0$: $\\bar{X}\\sim N\\left(500,\\,\\frac{8^2}{16}\\right)=N(500,\\,2^2)$.", "why": "The sample mean has variance $\\sigma^2/n$, so its standard deviation is $8/4=2$."}, {"do": "$P(\\bar{X}\\ge504.5)=P\\left(Z\\ge\\frac{504.5-500}{2}\\right)=P(Z\\ge2.25)=1-0.9878=0.0122$.", "why": "Standardise using the standard deviation of $\\bar{X}$. Use the upper tail because $504.5>500$."}, {"do": "Two-tailed p-value $=2\\times0.0122=0.0244$.", "why": "A mean equally far below 500 g would be just as extreme, so both tails count."}, {"do": "$0.0244<0.05$, so reject $H_0$.", "why": "The p-value is below the significance level, so this result is unlikely if $H_0$ 