}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is $0.0122$ doubled in the fourth step of the worked example?", "o": ["The sample is small, so the probability must be increased to be safe", "$H_1$ is two-tailed, so a mean equally far below 500 g would be just as extreme", "Doubling corrects for using $\\sigma$ instead of $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$ earlier", "Every p-value is doubled to make the test more cautious"], "a": 1, "f": ["Sample size is already allowed for through $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$. Doubling has nothing to do with a small sample.", "Right. Extreme results in either tail count against $H_0$, so you include both tails before comparing with 0.05.", "The standard deviation was already corrected in step 2. Doubling deals with the number of tails, not with $n$.", "Doubling is not routine. For a one-tailed test you compare the single-tail probability directly."]}, "faded": {"task": "Battery lives are Normally distributed with $\\sigma=12$ hours. The mean is claimed to be 80 hours. A random sample of 36 has mean 76.5 hours. Test at 5% whether the mean is lower than 80.", "given": ["$H_0:\\mu=80$, $H_1:\\mu<80$ (one-tailed, because 'lower')", "Under $H_0$: $\\bar{X}\\sim N(80,\\,2^2)$, since $12/\\sqrt{36}=2$"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Find $P\\left(Z\\le\\frac{76.5-80}{12}\\right)=P(Z\\le-0.29)$", "Find $P(\\bar{X}\\le76.5)=P(Z\\le-1.75)=0.9599$", "Find $P(\\bar{X}\\le76.5)=P(Z\\le-1.75)=1-0.9599=0.0401$", "Find $2\\times P(\\bar{X}\\le76.5)=2\\times0.0401=0.0802$"], "a": 2, "f": ["This divides by $\\sigma=12$ instead of the standard deviation of $\\bar{X}$, which is 2. That gives the wrong $z$-value.", "$\\Phi(1.75)=0.9599$ is the area above $-1.75$. The lower tail is $1-0.9599$, so you have the wrong tail.", "Right. $z=\\frac{76.5-80}{2}=-1.75$, and the lower-tail probability is $0.0401$. This is below 0.05, so you reject $H_0$.", "Doubling is for two-tailed tests only. $H_1$ here is $\\mu<80$, so use the single tail."]}, "trap": "Using $\\sigma$ instead of $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$ when standardising a sample mean. Write the distribution of $\\bar{X}$ under $H_0$ first, then standardise with its standard deviation.", "recap": "For a Normal mean test, use $\\bar{X}\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2/n)$ under $H_0$, find the p-value (doubled if two-tailed), compare, then conclude in context."}
